.TH Feraiseexcept 3 "2026-05-03" "Substrate" "Library Functions Manual"
.SH NAME
feraiseexcept \- raise floating-point exception flags
.SH LIBRARY
Math Library (libm, \-lm)
.SH SYNOPSIS
.nf
.B #include <fenv.h>
.PP
.B "int feraiseexcept( " int excepts " );"
.SH DESCRIPTION
The \fBferaiseexcept()\fR function raises the floating\-point exception
flags specified by the bitwise\-OR of
.BR excepts .
This simulates the occurrence of floating-point exceptions without performing
the actual operation.

The following exceptions may be raised:
.TP
.B FE_DIVBY_ERR
Division by zero.
.TP
.B FE_INVALID
Invalid operation (e.g., \fBsqrt(\-1)\fR).
.TP
.B FE_INEXACT
Result is not equal to the mathematical result.
.TP
.B FE_OVERFLOW
Result too large to be represented.
.TP
.B FE_UNDERFLOW
Result is small (subnormal or loss of precision).
.PP
When \fIFE_OVERFLOW\fR or \fIFE_UNDERFLOW\fR is raised, \fIFE_INEXACT\fR
is also raised automatically, as overflow and underflow are always accompanied
by a loss of precision.

.SH RETURN VALUE
The \fBferaiseexcept()\fR function returns zero on success.
.SH ERRORS
No errors are defined. The function does not set
.BR errno .
.SH EXAMPLES
.nf
#include <fenv.h>
#include <stdio.h>
#include <math.h>

int main(void) {
    /* Simulate raising an invalid operation exception */
    feraiseexcept(FE_INVALID);

    if (fetestexcept(FE_INVALID)) {
        printf("Invalid operation flag is set\\n");
    }

    /* Simulate overflow (which also implies inexact) */
    feraiseexcept(FE_OVERFLOW);

    if (fetestexcept(FE_INEXACT)) {
        printf("Inexact flag is implicitly set by overflow\\n");
    }
    return 0;
}
.fi
.SH SEE ALSO
\fBfenv\fR(7), \fBfeclearexcept\fR(3), \fBfegetexceptflag\fR(3), \fBfetestexcept\fR(3)
